3. Dependency Injection (DI)

24. What is Dependency Injection?
-->Simple explanation

Dependency Injection means Spring provides the required objects to a class instead of the class creating those objects itself.

For example, EmployeeService needs EmployeeRepository.

Without DI:
  class EmployeeService {
  
      EmployeeRepository repository = new EmployeeRepository();
  
  }
  
  Here, EmployeeService creates its dependency.

With DI:
    class EmployeeService {
    
        private EmployeeRepository repository;
    
        EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
            this.repository = repository;
        }
    }

Spring creates EmployeeRepository and gives it to EmployeeService.

Interview answer:
    Dependency Injection is a design principle in which an object's dependencies are provided by an external container
    rather than the object creating them itself.






25. Why is DI needed?
-->DI is mainly used to achieve loose coupling.

Without DI:

class PaymentService {
    private CreditCardPayment payment = new CreditCardPayment();
}

PaymentService is tightly coupled to CreditCardPayment.

With DI:

class PaymentService {

    private Payment payment;

    PaymentService(Payment payment) {
        this.payment = payment;
    }
}

Now we can provide:

CreditCardPayment

or

UPIPayment

without changing PaymentService.

Advantages
Loose coupling
Easier testing
Easier maintenance
Easier to change implementations
Better code reusability
Spring manages object creation and dependencies
Interview answer

DI is needed to reduce tight coupling between classes, improve testability and maintainability, and allow Spring to manage 
object creation and dependencies.






26. How DI is related to IoC?
-->Simple definitions:
IoC (Inversion of Control): 
        The control of creating and managing objects is given to an external system like the Spring container.
DI (Dependency Injection): 
        The required objects (dependencies) are provided to a class from outside instead of the class creating them itself.

Relationship :
        DI is a way of implementing IoC.
  

This is a very important interview question.

        IoC
        
        Inversion of Control is the broader principle.
        
        It means:
        
        Control of object creation and dependency management is transferred from the programmer to Spring.
        
        DI
        
        Dependency Injection is one way Spring implements IoC.
        
        Think:

                IoC
                 |
        -------------------
        |                 |
       DI          Other mechanisms
        |
  Constructor
  Setter
  Field


Example

Normally:

EmployeeService service = new EmployeeService();

The programmer controls object creation.

With Spring:

EmployeeService service

Spring creates and manages the object.

Interview answer

IoC is a principle where control is transferred to the Spring container, while Dependency Injection is the technique Spring uses
to implement IoC by injecting dependencies into objects.

⭐ Remember

IoC = Principle

DI = Technique






27. Types of Dependency Injection?
-->There are mainly three types:

1. Constructor Injection

Dependency is provided through the constructor.

class EmployeeService {

    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}
2. Setter Injection

Dependency is provided through a setter method.

class EmployeeService {

    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    public void setRepository(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}
3. Field Injection

Dependency is injected directly into a field.

class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repository;
}
Interview answer

The three common types of Dependency Injection are Constructor Injection, Setter Injection, and Field Injection.









28. Constructor Injection?
-->In Constructor Injection, dependencies are passed through the class constructor.

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

In modern Spring, if there is only one constructor, @Autowired is not required.

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private final EmployeeRepository repository;

    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Spring automatically injects EmployeeRepository.

Advantages
Dependency cannot easily be missing
Supports final fields
Good for immutable objects
Easy to test
Makes dependencies clearly visible









29. Setter Injection?
-->In Setter Injection, Spring injects the dependency using a setter method.

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Here Spring calls:

setRepository(repository);

and injects the dependency.

When useful?

Setter injection is useful when the dependency is optional or can be changed after object creation.








30. Field Injection?
-->Field Injection means Spring directly injects the dependency into a field.

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repository;
}

Spring finds the EmployeeRepository bean and places it into:

repository



Disadvantages :
    Field injection is generally discouraged because:
          Dependencies are hidden
          Difficult to unit test without Spring/reflection
          Cannot easily make dependency final
          Object can exist in an incompletely initialized state







31. Which injection is preferred and why?
-->⭐ Constructor Injection is preferred.

Example:

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private final EmployeeRepository repository;

    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}
Why?

Because it:
      Makes dependencies explicit
      Supports final
      Helps create immutable objects
      Makes unit testing easier
      Ensures required dependencies are provided during object creation
      Interview answer

Constructor Injection is generally preferred because it makes dependencies explicit, supports immutability using final fields, 
improves testability, and ensures required dependencies are available when the object is created.

⭐ Interview shortcut :
        Constructor → Preferred
        Setter      → Optional dependencies
        Field       → Generally discouraged








@Autowired Modes

32. What is @Autowired?
-->@Autowired tells Spring:

"Find a suitable bean and inject it here."

Example:
    @Component
    class EmployeeService {
    
        @Autowired
        private EmployeeRepository repository;
    }

Spring searches for an EmployeeRepository bean and injects it.

It can be used with:
      Constructor
      Setter
      Field
      Methods

Example constructor:
      @Autowired
      public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
          this.repository = repository;
      }







33. What is autowiring by type?
-->This is the important concept behind @Autowired.

Spring primarily looks for a bean based on its type.

Suppose:

@Repository
class MySQLRepository implements Repository {
}

and:

@Service
class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private Repository repository;
}

Spring searches:

Repository type
       ↓
MySQLRepository bean
       ↓
Inject

If there is exactly one matching bean, Spring can inject it.

Interview answer

Autowiring by type means Spring identifies a dependency based on its class or interface type and injects the matching bean.









34. What is autowiring by name?
-->Autowiring by name means matching the bean name with the dependency/property name.

For example:

@Component("mysqlRepository")
class MySQLRepository implements Repository {
}

And:

private Repository mysqlRepository;

The name:

mysqlRepository

matches the bean name.

Important clarification

With modern @Autowired, type matching is primary. If multiple beans match by type, Spring can use the injection point's name as a fallback for choosing among them.

So don't say:

"@Autowired always autowires by name."

That's incorrect.











35. What is autowiring by constructor?
-->Constructor autowiring means Spring resolves the dependencies required by a constructor and passes the corresponding beans to it.

Example:

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private final Repository repository;

    public EmployeeService(Repository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

If Spring has one Repository bean, it injects it.

Important

If a class has only one constructor, Spring can use it without @Autowired.

public EmployeeService(Repository repository) {
    this.repository = repository;
}

No annotation required.








36. What is @Qualifier?
-->@Qualifier is used when multiple beans of the same type exist and we want to specify exactly which bean should be injected.

Example:

interface Payment {
}

Two implementations:

@Component("creditCard")
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {
}
@Component("upi")
class UPIPayment implements Payment {
}

Now:

@Autowired
private Payment payment;

Spring sees:

Payment
 ├── CreditCardPayment
 └── UPIPayment

It doesn't know which one to choose.

So we use:

@Autowired
@Qualifier("upi")
private Payment payment;

Now Spring injects:

UPIPayment
Constructor example
public PaymentService(
        @Qualifier("upi") Payment payment) {
    this.payment = payment;
}
Interview answer

@Qualifier is used to resolve ambiguity when multiple beans of the same type are available by specifying the bean that should be injected.







37. What is @Primary?
-->@Primary tells Spring:

"If multiple beans match this type, prefer this bean by default."

Example:

@Component
@Primary
class UPIPayment implements Payment {
}

Another bean:

@Component
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {
}

Now:

@Autowired
private Payment payment;

Spring chooses:

UPIPayment

because it is marked:

@Primary
@Primary vs @Qualifier
| `@Primary`                      | `@Qualifier`                       |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Defines default preference      | Explicitly selects a bean          |
| Used when multiple beans exist  | Used when multiple beans exist     |
| Less specific                   | More specific                      |
| Good for default implementation | Good for a specific implementation |

⭐ Easy memory
@Primary   → "Choose this by default"
@Qualifier → "Choose exactly this"





38. What happens when multiple beans of same type exist?
-->Suppose:

interface Payment {}

We have:

@Component
class UPIPayment implements Payment {}
@Component
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {}

And:

@Autowired
private Payment payment;

Spring finds:

Payment
   ↓
2 matching beans
   ↓
Ambiguous

Spring cannot decide which bean to inject and normally throws:

NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException
How can we solve it?
Option 1: @Primary
@Primary
@Component
class UPIPayment implements Payment {
}
Option 2: @Qualifier
@Autowired
@Qualifier("creditCardPayment")
private Payment payment;
Option 3: Inject a collection
@Autowired
private List<Payment> payments;

Then Spring can inject all matching beans.






39. What is circular dependency?
-->Circular dependency occurs when two or more beans depend on each other.

Example:

EmployeeService
      ↓
EmployeeRepository
      ↓
EmployeeService

Or:

class A {
    B b;
}

and:

class B {
    A a;
}

So:

A needs B
B needs A

This is called circular dependency.

Constructor example
@Component
class A {

    private final B b;

    A(B b) {
        this.b = b;
    }
}
@Component
class B {

    private final A a;

    B(A a) {
        this.a = a;
    }
}

Spring cannot create either bean because:

Create A
 ↓
Need B
 ↓
Create B
 ↓
Need A
 ↓
A is not created yet

This generally results in a bean creation failure.

Best solution

Avoid circular dependencies by redesigning the classes.

Often you can:
    Move shared logic into another service
    Reduce unnecessary dependencies
    Introduce an intermediate component








40. How Spring resolves dependencies?
-->This is a very important overall question.

When Spring creates a bean, it checks its dependencies and tries to find suitable beans in the ApplicationContext.

For example:

@Component
class EmployeeService {

    private final Repository repository;

    EmployeeService(Repository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Spring roughly does:

1. Find EmployeeService bean
          ↓
2. See it requires Repository
          ↓
3. Search ApplicationContext
          ↓
4. Find matching Repository bean
          ↓
5. Resolve ambiguity if necessary
   (@Primary / @Qualifier)
          ↓
6. Create EmployeeService
          ↓
7. Inject Repository


Interview answer :
    Spring resolves dependencies by examining the injection point, finding matching beans in the ApplicationContext, and using 
    mechanisms such as type matching, @Qualifier, and @Primary to select the appropriate bean.







41. How to inject primitive values?
-->Spring can inject values such as:

int
String
double
boolean

using @Value.

Example:

@Component
class Employee {

    @Value("25")
    private int age;

    @Value("Karthikeya")
    private String name;
}

Spring injects:

age  = 25
name = Karthikeya
More commonly, values come from properties

application.properties:

employee.name=Karthikeya
employee.age=25

Then:

@Component
class Employee {

    @Value("${employee.name}")
    private String name;

    @Value("${employee.age}")
    private int age;
}

Spring reads the values from the configuration and injects them.

Important

@Autowired is primarily for beans/objects.

@Value is commonly used for configuration values/primitives/String values.

